  "narrative": "All returned estimates are already signed to the registered claim direction, so positive signed estimates support the registered hypothesis and negative signed estimates oppose it. For signed CRISPR dependency scores, I continued to use an approximate 0.1 absolute signed-score difference as a practical benchmark for scientific meaningfulness, because relative changes are unstable when scores are centered near zero; the supplied 10% relative threshold is also noted, but the absolute benchmark is more interpretable here.  \n\nThe batch-A context checks are all strongly supportive. H140 gives dependency_SMARCA2 smarca4_loss in batch A as +0.747 (95% CI +0.709 to +0.785; n=113/406), H141 gives dependency_NFE2L2 keap1_loss in batch A as +0.812 (95% CI +0.765 to +0.858; n=102/417), H142 gives dependency_USP7 tp53_loss in batch A as +0.417 (95% CI +0.284 to +0.550; n=112/407), and H143 gives dependency_STAT3 pten_loss in batch A as +0.234 (95% CI +0.125 to +0.342; n=63/456). Each interval lies entirely above the practical benchmark and excludes zero in the registered direction. I therefore accept these as dataset-specific robustness/context results and close them. They do not establish causality or external generalizability, but they argue against batch A being an obvious attenuation boundary for these accepted axes. H146 completes the main library context for MSI-high/WRN within Avana: +0.794 (95% CI +0.764 to +0.825; n=120/1645), also far above benchmark, accepted and closed.  \n\nThe high-MYC-expression interaction checks are generally small and do not support meaningful subgroup modification. H126 (MSI-high/WRN interaction in high MYC expression) is -0.021 (95% CI -0.080 to +0.038), opposing the claimed direction and far below the practical magnitude. H127 (SMARCA4 loss/SMARCA2 interaction in high MYC expression) is +0.022 (95% CI -0.017 to +0.061), H128 (KEAP1 loss/NFE2L2 interaction in high MYC expression) is +0.040 (95% CI -0.00025 to +0.080), and H1